  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday Night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1145 AM CDT Thu Jun 25 2026\n\nChances for showers and thunderstorms will continue to increase \ninto the weekend, peaking Saturday at 75-90% across northern zones\nand 45- 70% to the south. Saturday and Sunday continue to look \ninteresting in the guidance, as ongoing WAA will bring abundant \ninstability into the region, with steep lapse rates. Being so \ndisplaced from the forcing, like we will be this weekend, is a \ndisadvantage when it comes to severe weather. Despite the presence\nof a warm front, the forcing from this is expected to be \nrelatively weak. A marginal risk (level 1 out of 5) is in place \nfor middle Tennessee again on Saturday, with weak shear putting a \ndamper on hazards except for damaging winds (again). We could see \nan isolated severe storm Sunday as well, but right now I think \nthat risk will be lower than Saturday.\n\nA high pressure system builds right over the southeastern United \nStates Sunday into Monday, intensifying the heat. Highs will get \ninto the mid to upper 90s by mid-week next week, with heat indices \nin the triple digits. Heat risk looks to be in the major category to \npossibly extreme towards the end of the period, meaning heat illness \nwill occur in a shorter amount of time outdoors than usual. Make \nsure to keep this in mind if you have any outdoor activities in the \ncoming days, and visit weather.gov/ohx/heat for more information on heat \nsafety.",
